diff --git a/docs/self-hosting.md b/docs/self-hosting.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3ff7bb6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/self-hosting.md
@@ -0,0 +1,75 @@
+# Self-hosting Fizzy on one server
+
+This guide deploys Fizzy to a single Linux server with [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org). The whole app runs in one container: the web server, the background jobs (Solid Queue runs inside Puma), and the SQLite databases. kamal-proxy terminates TLS with a Let's Encrypt certificate.
+
+## What you need
+
+- A Linux server you can reach over SSH as `root`. To use another user, set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml`. `kamal setup` installs Docker on the server if it's missing.
+- A DNS record that points your hostname at the server, with ports 80 and 443 open. Let's Encrypt needs both.
+- Docker on your own machine, to build the image. Kamal starts a small registry container on your machine and pushes the image there, so you don't need a registry account.
+- An SMTP server that Fizzy can send through. Fizzy signs people in with a code sent by email, so email has to work.
+
+## 1. Configure
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml` and change the values marked `CHANGE ME`:
+
+- `app_host`, the hostname, and `server`, the server's address
+- `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`, the From address on outgoing mail
+- `SMTP_ADDRESS` and `SMTP_USERNAME`, for your mail server. Leave `SMTP_USERNAME` empty if the server doesn't require a login.
+
+Also check these, which aren't marked:
+
+- `SMTP_PORT` and `SMTP_TLS`. Port 587 uses STARTTLS (`SMTP_TLS: "false"`). Port 465 uses implicit TLS (`SMTP_TLS: "true"`).
+- `builder.arch`. It's `amd64`, so change it if your server is ARM.
+
+## 2. Secrets
+
+`.kamal/secrets` tells Kamal which values to pass to the container. It only holds references to environment variables, so it's safe to commit. Export the values in your shell before you run Kamal, and keep them in your password manager:
+
+- `SECRET_KEY_BASE`: required. Create it once with `bin/rails secret`. Keep it stable, because changing it signs every user out.
+- `SMTP_PASSWORD`: your SMTP password. Leave it empty if the server doesn't need a login.
+- `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`: optional, for browser push notifications. The file shows how to create a pair.
+
+Kamal passes an empty value through without complaint, so check that `SECRET_KEY_BASE` is exported. If it isn't, the container refuses to boot and the deploy fails with a `secret_key_base` error.
+
+## 3. Set up the server
+
+```sh
+bundle install
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+`kamal setup` installs Docker, builds the image, starts kamal-proxy, and boots Fizzy. The first boot creates the databases, so it can take a minute.
+
+When it's done, open `https://<your-host>` and enter your email address. Use the code from the email to sign in. On an empty installation, the first sign-in creates the account, asks for your name, and makes you its owner. After that, other people join with a join link from the account settings.
+
+## 4. Update
+
+To release a new version, update the code and run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Deploys replace the container. They don't touch the data.
+
+## Where your data lives
+
+Everything Fizzy writes goes to `/rails/storage` in the container. That path is the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume on the server. It holds the SQLite databases (the main database, plus the queue, cache, and cable databases) and any uploaded files.
+
+- `bin/kamal deploy` and `bin/kamal remove` don't delete the volume.
+- Back up the volume regularly. Copy running SQLite databases with SQLite's own backup (the `.backup` command), not a plain file copy, which can catch the database mid-write.
+
+## Things to know
+
+- During a deploy, the old and new containers briefly run side by side and share the volume. The new one runs any pending migrations when it starts.
+- Background jobs run in the web container, not in a separate worker. Size `WEB_CONCURRENCY` and `JOB_CONCURRENCY` in `config/deploy.yml` for the server's memory.
+- Sign-in codes are sent by background jobs. If they don't arrive, check the SMTP settings in `config/deploy.yml`, and the logs.
+
+## Everyday commands
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app logs -f   # follow the logs
+bin/kamal console       # Rails console in the running container
+bin/kamal details       # status of the containers
+```
diff --git a/test/config/deploy_config_test.rb b/test/config/deploy_config_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a36aafa
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/config/deploy_config_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "erb"
+require "kamal"
+
+# config/deploy.yml is what `bin/kamal setup` reads on a fresh fork of the
+# one-server setup. These checks keep it consistent with the rest of the app.
+class DeployConfigTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  DEPLOY_FILE = Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml")
+  SECRETS_FILE = Rails.root.join(".kamal/secrets")
+
+  setup do
+    @config = YAML.safe_load(ERB.new(DEPLOY_FILE.read).result)
+  end
+
+  test "Kamal accepts the deploy config" do
+    kamal_config = Kamal::Configuration.create_from(config_file: DEPLOY_FILE)
+
+    assert_equal "fizzy", kamal_config.service
+  end
+
+  test "web and background jobs run in one container" do
+    assert_equal [ "web" ], @config["servers"].keys
+    assert_equal true, @config.dig("env", "clear", "SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA")
+  end
+
+  test "the SQLite databases and uploads live in a volume" do
+    assert_includes @config["volumes"], "fizzy_storage:/rails/storage"
+  end
+
+  test "TLS is terminated by the proxy for the app host" do
+    assert @config.dig("proxy", "ssl")
+    assert_equal @config.dig("proxy", "host"), @config.dig("env", "clear", "APP_HOST")
+  end
+
+  test "every secret the deploy asks for is listed in .kamal/secrets" do
+    listed = SECRETS_FILE.read.scan(/^([A-Z0-9_]+)=/).flatten
+
+    assert_empty @config.dig("env", "secret") - listed
+  end
+end
